title: 'pallet-revive: bound per-transaction receipt size'
doc:
- audience: Runtime User
  description: |-
    The logs of one Ethereum transaction may now take up at most 2,097,152 bytes (2 MiB) of its
    receipt (`limits::MAX_RECEIPT_LOGS_BYTES`). The size is the RLP encoding of every log the
    transaction keeps: address, topics and data, each with its RLP headers. It counts the logs of
    every contract the transaction calls and the logs the runtime mirrors, such as ERC-20
    `Transfer` logs for asset balance changes. The limit applies to `eth_transact` and
    `eth_substrate_call`, and to their dry runs and gas estimation, so an estimate reports the same
    outcome as inclusion. Calls that do not run as an Ethereum transaction, such as `Revive::call`,
    have no receipt and are not affected.

    An Ethereum transaction at the EIP-7825 cap of 2^24 gas can log at most about 2.09 MB. This
    chain enforces neither that cap nor the `LOG` price of 8 gas per byte, so a transaction whose
    logs went past 2 MiB could succeed before and now fails with `ReceiptTooLarge`. This applies
    to contracts deployed before the change as well. Split such work over several transactions.

    A log that would take the receipt past the limit fails the call frame that emitted it with the
    new `ReceiptTooLarge` error, the same way an oversized event or too many topics fail it. This
    includes a `LOG` instruction, the `deposit_event` syscall, a precompile's event and a log the
    runtime mirrors inside the frame, for example the `Transfer` of an asset precompile call. The
    frame's state changes and logs roll back, and the calling contract can handle the failure.
    A log mirrored outside any frame, such as for a storage deposit paid in an asset after the
    outermost call returned, fails the whole transaction instead. A failed transaction is
    included with a failed receipt: its state changes roll back, storage deposits are rolled
    back, and it pays the fee for the weight it used.

    Mirrored logs emitted outside any Ethereum transaction are collected into the block's
    synthetic transaction, whose receipt has the same limit. A log that does not fit there stays a
    substrate-only `ContractEmitted` event, as a log past `MaxOutsideFrameLogs` already does.

    The RLP encoded payload of an `eth_transact` may now be at most 1,245,184 bytes
    (`limits::MAX_TRANSACTION_PAYLOAD_SIZE`: 1 MiB of code, 128 KiB of call data and 64 KiB for the
    rest of the transaction). A larger payload is rejected with
    `InvalidTransaction::ExhaustsResources` when the transaction is checked, and a dry run of it
    fails with the same error. Transactions within the code and call data limits are not affected
    unless they also carry more than about 64 KiB of access or authorization lists.

    The payload limit also applies to an `eth_transact` to `RUNTIME_PALLETS_ADDR`, whose call data
    is a SCALE encoded runtime call and is not bound by the call data limit otherwise. A large
    `utility::batch_all` or `preimage::note_preimage` sent that way is now rejected; submit it as a
    native extrinsic instead. Dev chains with `DebugEnabled` and
    `DebugSettings::allow_unlimited_contract_size` can no longer submit EVM init code that takes the
    payload past the limit.
- audience: Runtime Dev
  description: |-
    `PrecompileExt::deposit_event` now returns `Result<(), DispatchError>`. It fails with
    `Error::ReceiptTooLarge` when the log does not fit the open receipt, and deposits nothing in
    that case. The trait is sealed, so no implementation changes. Precompiles that emit logs
    through it should propagate the result, so that the precompile call fails with the error:

    ```rust
    // before
    env.deposit_event(topics, data);
    // after
    env.deposit_event(topics, data)?;
    ```

    Ignoring the result compiles with an unused `Result` warning and does not keep the log: the
    frame then fails with `ReceiptTooLarge` when it returns.

    `Error` has the new variant `ReceiptTooLarge` (index `0x45`). Code that matches on the error
    exhaustively needs an arm for it.

    `limits::MAX_TRANSACTION_PAYLOAD_SIZE` grows by 64 KiB and is now enforced on `eth_transact`
    payloads, see above. `integrity_test` sizes the Ethereum block builder from this limit and the
    receipt limit, instead of from the block weight. No storage migration and no configuration
    change is needed.
crates:
- name: pallet-revive
  bump: major
- name: pallet-revive-fixtures
  bump: minor
- name: pallet-revive-uapi
  bump: none
- name: pallet-assets-precompiles
  bump: patch
- name: asset-hub-westend-runtime
  bump: patch
